Removing the Last Set Bit :

Approach:

To remove the rightmost set bit (rightmost 1) of a number, use:

n & (n - 1)
Key Idea:

When we subtract 1 from a number:

The rightmost set bit (1) becomes 0.
All bits after it (which are 0) become 1.
Other bits remain the same.

After performing AND (&), the rightmost set bit gets removed.

Example:
n = 13

Binary:

13 = 1101

Subtract 1:

13 - 1 = 12

1101
-  1
----
1100

Now perform AND:

  1101
& 1100
------
  1100

Result:

1100 = 12

The last set bit (1) has been removed.

Why does n & (n-1) work?

Example:

n = 101100

Here the rightmost set bit is:

101100
   ↑

Now:

n - 1 = 101011

AND operation:

  101100
& 101011
--------
  101000

The rightmost 1 is removed.

Common Use:
This operation is mainly used in * Brian Kernighan's Algorithm * to count set bits.

Example:

while(n) {
    n = n & (n - 1);
    count++;
}

Each iteration removes one set bit.

Time Complexity: O(1)
Only subtraction and one AND operation are performed.

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra space is used.

Code:
class Solution {
public:
    int removeLastSetBit(int n) {
        return n & (n - 1);
    }
};

💡 Shortcut to remember:

n & (n-1) → removes the rightmost 1 bit

Example:

1101 → 1100
1100 → 1000
1000 → 0000

Each operation removes one set bit.
